<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yanam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yanam is a small town on the Godavari delta in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, enclosed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>. A French trading post from the 1720s, it remained part of French India until 1954, when it was transferred de facto to India, a transfer made legal in 1962. It is now a district of the Union Territory of Puducherry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yanam.jpg" alt="Yanam" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anand Deepak Racharla · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gowthami_river_Godavari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yanam lies in the delta of the Godavari River, at the confluence of the Koringa River, nine kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> on the Circar coast. The town is entirely surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>. The Bank Canal, which begins at the Dowleswaram headworks of Sir Arthur Cotton's barrage on the Godavari, brings irrigation and drinking water; it is popularly called the French Channel because it was built under a 1949 agreement between the French government and the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The French established a trading post at Yanaon in 1723, the third French colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, but gave it up in 1727 after commercial operations proved unsuccessful. Governor-General Dupleix seized it again in 1731, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> confirmed the French hold in 1751. Until the end of the Napoleonic wars Yanam passed intermittently under British control, and it was finally restored to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1814. Its Tuesday market drew Telugu people of the Madras Presidency, who came to buy foreign and smuggled goods.</p><p>On 13 June 1954 a coup in Yanam joined it to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The de facto transfer of the four enclaves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a>, Yanam, Mahé and Karikal took place on 1 November 1954. A treaty of cession was signed on 28 May 1956, but the legal transfer waited until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a>: the French parliament ratified the treaty in May 1962, and on 16 August 1962 the instruments of ratification were exchanged. The enclaves became the Union Territory of Pondicherry on 7 January 1963. After the liberation about 10,000 people in Yanam chose French nationality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Yanam is a district of the Union Territory of Puducherry. The 1956 treaty kept the former colonies' special administrative status, and French remains an official language of the territory alongside Telugu and English. The town blends French and Telugu culture, which gave it the nickname Frelugu. Its population was 31,362 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2001. Among its monuments is the Vishnalayam, a 15th-century temple to Venkateswara.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yanam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Further settlements followed at Chandernagore in Bengal, Mahe on the Malabar coast, Karaikal and <b>Yanam</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-india">French India</a></li></ul></section>
